---
/**
 * The first screen of a page a stranger arrives on (kolonie-website#121,
 * `#123`, `#128`).
 *
 * **Three issues asked for the same thing on five pages**, which is why this is
 * a component rather than five paragraphs. `#121` wants `/academy/` to say what
 * an operator's agent gains before the graph; `#123` wants `/the-register/` and
 * `/run-a-swarm/` to open in the homepage's story rather than in the Colony's
 * vocabulary; `#128` wants `/pricing/` and `/quests/` to say where the money
 * sits before either page's detail. Written five times, that is five ledes that
 * drift apart by January.
 *
 * **It landed on four of them, and `/quests/` is the one it should not be on**
 * (`#128`). That page is documentation and is read by somebody who has already
 * decided; a first screen written to convince them is aimed at a reader who is
 * not there. `#128`'s bridge is a paragraph of reference at the top of it
 * instead. The count is worth recording because it is the answer to *why not
 * simply every page*: a lede is what a page needs when a stranger lands on it,
 * which is a property of the page rather than of the site. `LEDE_PAGES` in
 * `lede.built-test.ts` is the list, and appending to it is the whole of
 * deciding a page has one.
 *
 * **What it enforces is placement, not wording.** The rules it exists to serve
 * are `AGENTS.md` §3 — an outcome for the human first, a Colony term never
 * standing on its own, install mechanics never outranking the reason. Those are
 * properties of sentences and no component can check them. What a component can
 * do is give the first screen a name in the markup, so `lede.built-test.ts` can
 * assert that every persuasion page has one and that it comes before the page's
 * first Colony word. That test is the thing `#125` was written to make
 * possible, and it is what this class name is for.
 *
 * ## What it deliberately does not carry
 *
 * **No button.** `layers.built-test.ts` enforces that a documentation page
 * carries no call to action, and `/quests/` is documentation by `chrome.ts`'s
 * exception list. A component with an `action` prop would either break that
 * rule wherever it reached such a page or grow a flag for which pages are
 * allowed to use it, and the flag is the classification `chrome.ts` already
 * owns. `#128` then decided `/quests/` gets prose rather than this block, so
 * today's four routes are all persuasion — but that is where the list happens
 * to stand, not a property of the component, and a button would make the next
 * documentation lede impossible rather than merely awkward. The link onward is written into the prose at the call site, where the
 * sentence around it can say why a reader would follow it.
 *
 * **No title, no icon, no eyebrow.** The frontmatter `title` is already the
 * page's `h1`, rendered by `[...slug].astro`, and a heading between the two
 * would be the page announcing itself twice.
 *
 * **Not `PlainTerm`, and the two do not compete.** That component is one
 * sentence carrying one term at the point the term is unavoidable. This one is
 * the block a page opens with, and the ordinary case is that it contains no
 * term at all — a lede needing three glosses is a lede written in the wrong
 * language.
 */
---

<div class="lede">
  <slot />
</div>

<style>
  /* Tokens only — no colour value is written outside theme.css, and
     src/styles/theme.test.ts fails the build if one appears here. */
  .lede {
    /* `--k-measure-lede` is narrower than the body measure and was already the
       landing page's answer to the same question (`index.astro`). A first screen
       is read at a glance rather than settled into, and a short line is what
       makes that possible. */
    max-width: var(--k-measure-lede);
    margin-block-end: var(--k-flow-section);
    padding-block-end: var(--k-flow);
    border-block-end: 1px solid var(--k-hairline);
  }

  /* One step up from body copy and no more. The lede is distinguished by being
     first and by being short; setting it two steps up turns it into a pull quote
     and pushes the page it introduces off the phone screen it was written for. */
  .lede :global(p) {
    font-size: var(--k-text-lg);
    line-height: var(--k-leading);
  }

  .lede :global(p + p) {
    margin-block-start: var(--k-flow-tight);
  }

  .lede :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-block-end: 0;
  }
</style>
